#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Steam Play on the Mac: Windows games that Mac Steam installed, played from Steam's library.

    macos/steamplay list              Windows games in Steam's libraries
    macos/steamplay sync [--dry-run] [--remove]
                                      give each one a shortcut in Steam's library, or take the
                                      shortcuts out again (Steam quit)
    macos/steamplay play <app id>     start one now, through Steam Play.app
    macos/steamplay hook [--dry-run] [--remove] [app id...]
                                      make each one's own library entry start it (Steam quit)
    macos/steamplay setup [--dry-run] set Steam Play up: the tools, Steam's platform switch and a
                                      compatibility tool for every game (Steam quit)
    macos/steamplay status            the switch, the tools and each installed game's choice
    macos/steamplay prefer <app id> native|proton|auto
                                      your own choice for one game (Steam quit)
    macos/steamplay guard             put right what would make Steam remove installs (see below)
    macos/steamplay guard install|remove
                                      have that done by itself, as a login item of this user
    macos/steamplay tools             install the compatibility tools only (Steam quit)
    macos/steamplay links [app id...] put back the symbolic links Steam left out of a Mac build
    macos/steamplay map <app id> <tool|none> [<app id> <tool|none>...]
                                      write a game's compatibility tool directly (Steam quit)

Mac Steam downloads a Windows build when its download platform is forced, but will not launch
it. "sync" adds a non-Steam shortcut per game to the signed-in user's shortcuts.vdf, named
after the game and with its artwork from Steam's library cache, which opens
"Steam Play.app" (macos/make-steamplay-app.sh) for the game's app id. The game itself then
reports the real app id to Steam through Proton's lsteamclient, so Steam shows the game as
playing and counts its playtime. Shortcuts made earlier by sync are replaced, others are kept,
and shortcuts.vdf is backed up first. Steam must be quit while it is written, since Steam
rewrites the file when it quits.

"hook" is the other way to a Play button, without a second entry per game: it sets the game's
launch option, in the signed-in user's localconfig.vdf, to macos/steamplay-launch followed by
%command%, so that Steam's own Play starts the game through that script (which see). A launch
option the user already has stays, after %command%; one with its own %command% is left alone.
With app ids it does those games whatever is in their folders, without them every Windows game
"list" shows; --remove takes the script out again. Steam only offers Play for a Windows game
while it takes the Mac for a Windows machine.

"setup" is Steam Play's own way, and the one to use: Mac Steam has Linux's Steam Play, which it
runs only while it takes the Mac for a Linux machine (@sSteamCmdForcePlatformType linux in the
steam_dev.cfg next to its executables). Each game then goes by its compatibility tool, and Steam
does the rest itself: the right depots, Play, the prefix path, cloud saves. The two tools are in
macos/steamplay-tools: proton-macos runs a game's Windows build through Proton, macos-native makes
Steam install the Mac build and runs it unchanged. The rule, from what Steam's app cache says of
each game: a Mac build that runs on this Mac (64-bit or Apple Silicon) wins; every other game
gets Proton (a 32-bit Mac build cannot run here, and a Linux build must not be chosen). "prefer"
overrides the rule for one game and is remembered in choices.json (see STEAMPLAY_HOME).
"setup" installs the tools into /usr/local/share/steam/compatibilitytools.d (one of the few folders
Steam looks in, and the one no Steam update touches), writes the switch, maps the games in
config.vdf (CompatToolMapping; app id 0 is the default for games it does not know yet) and takes
"hook"'s launch options out. Run it again after buying games: a new game with a Mac or Linux
build needs its own mapping. WARNING: with the switch on linux and the tools missing, or the
switch gone, Steam takes Windows installs for wrong and removes them at its next start: "status"
says so, and "setup" puts both right. "tools" and "map" are the single steps.

"guard" is the protection against that. Quietly, and as often as it is run: it writes the switch
back if it is gone, installs the tools again if they are missing and, while Steam is not running,
maps games that are not mapped yet (new purchases). If Steam is running without Steam Play (its
compatibility log shows our tools ignored or never registered), installs are seconds from being
removed: it tells the user, stops Steam, repairs and starts Steam again. "guard install" copies
this script and the tools to ~/Library/Application Support/SteamPlay, on the internal disk, where
a background job may read them, and adds a LaunchAgent that keeps "guard watch" running from
login on: a small process that looks every two seconds and runs the guard when the switch or a
tool is gone, when Steam starts (its compatibility log grows) or its settings change, and every
five minutes. It logs what it did to ~/Library/Logs/SteamPlay/guard.log.

"links" mends what the switch breaks in a Mac build: while Steam takes the Mac for a Linux
machine it installs a Mac depot without the symbolic links the depot lists, and an app bundle
whose frameworks lack theirs does not start (dyld: Library not loaded). It reads the links from
the depot manifests Steam keeps in its depotcache and makes the ones that are missing, inside
the game's folder only. Without app ids it does every installed game mapped to macos-native;
the macos-native tool runs it for its game before each start.

Environment: STEAM_ROOT (default ~/Library/Application Support/Steam), STEAMPLAY_APP (default
<build root>/steamplay-apps/Steam Play.app), STEAMPLAY_HOME (default ~/Library/Application
Support/SteamPlay: choices.json, backups of what is written, the guard's copy),
STEAMPLAY_LAUNCH (default steamplay-launch next to this script), and STEAMPLAY_FAKE_STEAM=1 to try
sync or hook on a copy of a Steam folder while Steam runs.
"""
